  // ===================== ARTICLES =====================
  var H3 = 'class="font-display font-bold text-xl mt-6 mb-2 text-gold"';
  var articles = [
    { id: 27, slug: 'nyege-nyege-2026-wakaliwood-edition-preview', category: 'News', title: 'Nyege Nyege 2026: a river, seven stages and a live film set', excerpt: 'Uganda\'s flagship festival returns to Kalagala Falls on November 19-22 with 200-plus artists, a Wakaliwood collaboration, the Reggae and Boiler Room stages back, and a conference week leading in.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: 'images/articles/nyege-nyege-2026-nile.jpg', imgCaption: 'A stage on the Nile at Kalagala Falls (illustration: 7H Music Blog)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
      body: '<p class="lead">Nyege Nyege is going back to the river — and this year it is taking a film crew with it.</p> <p>Uganda\'s flagship festival returns to Adrift Overland Camp at Kalagala Falls, near Jinja, for its 11th edition on <strong>November 19-22</strong>: more than 200 artists across seven stages, four days and three nights on the Nile. Tickets went live in September at a launch held at the Talent Africa rooftop in Kampala, where organisers confirmed the riverside site as the festival\'s permanent home and set out a wider programme around the music — film, fashion, visual art, theatre, comedy and food.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The Wakaliwood Edition</h3> <p>This year\'s theme is a collaboration with <strong>Wakaliwood</strong>, the DIY action-film studio from Wakaliga that has spent two decades turning Kampala suburbs into action epics. Parts of the festival site will become a live film set, with scenes shot across the four days — a first dedicated film component, and a natural pairing for two Ugandan projects that built international audiences from fiercely independent roots.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>What is back, and what is new</h3> <p>The Reggae Stage and the Boiler Room both return, alongside dedicated Kenyan and Rwandan takeover showcases and the SO UG Stage powered by Uganda Waragi, which keeps its focus on contemporary Ugandan popular culture. Names announced for the main stage so far include <strong>King Saha</strong>, <strong>Jackie Chandiru</strong>, <strong>Linus</strong>, <strong>Abeeka Band</strong>, <strong>Etania</strong>, <strong>DJ Akio</strong> of Strictly Soul and the Zonke Africa DJ Collective. Uganda Waragi\'s maker, Uganda Breweries Limited, renewed its partnership on a three-year agreement, and a Ministry of Tourism tie-in under the Explore Uganda brand links the festival to regional travel packages and circuit destinations.</p> <p>Before the gates open, the second <strong>African Electronic Music Conference</strong> runs from November 17 to 19 in Jinja, turning festival week into a full gathering for the continent\'s electronic scene. In the weeks before that, warm-up parties travel to Nairobi and Kigali, hosted by Sheila Gashumba and Kenya\'s Azeezah Hashim.</p> <div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">Nyege Nyege 2026</p><ul><li><span class="k">When</span><span class="v">November 19-22, 2026</span></li><li><span class="k">Where</span><span class="v">Adrift Overland Camp, Kalagala Falls, Jinja</span></li><li><span class="k">Scale</span><span class="v">200+ artists · 7 stages · riverside camping for ~1,000</span></li><li><span class="k">Early bird</span><span class="v">UGX 100,000 "Die Hard" pass, ended September 30</span></li><li><span class="k">Tickets</span><span class="v">festival.nyegenyege.com — official sellers MTN MoMo and Tag Ticketing</span></li></ul></div> <p>It is worth remembering what the festival is worth where it happens: a government report released in 2025 put the 2024 edition\'s contribution to Jinja\'s economy at about <strong>USD 1.84 million (UGX 6.62 billion)</strong>. With camping expanded and tourism packages attached, the 2026 edition is being built to push that further.</p> <p class="note">Check the official site for current ticket tiers and the full line-up — names are still being announced. <em>Credit: Nyege Nyege Festival; SoftPower News; Daily Monitor; Travel PR News; RioTimes.</em></p>' },
    { id: 28, slug: 'joshua-baraka-tiwa-savage-commando', category: 'News', title: 'Joshua Baraka pulls "Commando" forward — the Tiwa Savage link-up lands this month', excerpt: 'The Recess II track he teased with Tiwa Savage is coming in October instead of with the EP, after a reception he calls "crazy" — and it is the clearest sign yet of Uganda-to-Lagos traffic.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '4 min read', img: 'images/articles/joshua-baraka-brakaland.jpg', imgCaption: 'Joshua Baraka on the Brakaland Tour (photo: Nile Post)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
      body: '<p class="lead">The biggest Ugandan record of the season may have just moved up a month.</p> <p>Joshua Baraka has been teasing "Commando", his collaboration with Nigerian star <strong>Tiwa Savage</strong>, and on October 11 he told fans it is arriving sooner than planned. "The reception on Commando is crazy.??? I\'m going to release it in October instead of November with the project," he posted on X. "Thank you Natives for always giving me the confidence when I walk into a room anywhere in the world." The teaser itself — captioned "ARE YOU READY?🔥..COMMANDO 🇺🇬X🇳🇬 B\'raka X Tiwa" — has since travelled across Ugandan and Nigerian music pages, and the song sits at number two on <em>Recess II</em>, the EP whose Spotify pre-save countdown still points to <strong>November 12</strong>.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Why it matters</h3> <p>Tiwa Savage is one of the defining voices of Afrobeats\' global decade, with a catalogue and a following far beyond Lagos. For Baraka the link-up extends a run that already includes the JAE5-produced "Wrong Places" (past 29 million Spotify plays on our last check) and a verse on South African amapiano producer Al Xapo\'s "SNOKONOKO VI" earlier this year. Uganda-to-Lagos traffic of this kind is still rarer than it should be, which makes the timing louder than the song.</p> <p>Two of the EP\'s other tracks, "YoYo" and "what do i know", are already out, and the homecoming <strong>B\'RAKA LAND</strong> concert at Lugogo Hockey Grounds falls sixteen days after the EP\'s November 12 date (see <a href="#article/joshua-baraka-recess-ii-braka-land">the Recess II story</a>).</p> <p class="note">No release date for the single has been confirmed beyond Baraka\'s own post — watch his channels. <em>Credit: X/@itsJoshuaBaraka; Sqoop; RN0nline; Patro Uganda; Spotify.</em></p>' },
    { id: 29, slug: 'kampala-live-season-chameleone-levixone-mesach', category: 'Events', title: 'Kampala\'s live season: Chameleone, Levixone, Mesach Semakula and a calendar that runs to December', excerpt: 'Anniversary shows, gospel nights and comeback concerts — plus jazz and comedy — as Uganda\'s promoters stack the last quarter of 2026.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: 'images/articles/kampala-live-season.jpg', imgCaption: 'A packed Kampala concert night (illustration: 7H Music Blog)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
      body: '<p class="lead">The last quarter of 2026 is the busiest Uganda\'s live circuit has looked in years.</p> <p>Start with October: the <strong>Kampala Jazz & Soul Benefit</strong> takes over the UMA Multi-Purpose Hall at the Show Grounds on <strong>October 24</strong>, doors at 5pm through midnight — one of the city\'s few dedicated jazz-and-soul nights this season. Around it, the month has already delivered Edwizzy Fest, AFROBEEZA\'s Afrobeats and Afro-house night at Acacia Mall, and a silent-disco series in Kimbeja, while ObaFest turned Gulu into a two-day entertainment hub up north.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>November: gospel, kadongo kamu, and the Nile</h3> <p><strong>Levixone</strong> brings A One Tym Experience to the Kampala Serena Hotel on <strong>November 8</strong>; <strong>Mesach Semakula</strong> celebrates 50 Years of Greatness at Kingdom Kampala on <strong>November 13</strong>; and <strong>Racheal Magoola</strong>\'s 60th-anniversary concert lands on <strong>November 27</strong>. Between those dates sits the one every calendar is built around — Nyege Nyege at Kalagala Falls, November 19-22 (<a href="#article/nyege-nyege-2026-wakaliwood-edition-preview">previewed here</a>) — with the African Electronic Music Conference running in Jinja from the 17th.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>December: the comeback shows</h3> <p><strong>Jose Chameleone</strong> returns to the Cricket Oval at Lugogo on <strong>December 4</strong> for "Unlimited" — and he has said publicly that he invited Presidents Samia Suluhu Hassan of Tanzania and Évariste Ndayishimiye of Burundi to be in the house. A week later <strong>Nina Roz</strong> headlines the Lugogo Hockey Grounds (December 11), and Afro Netion\'s Wave Edition closes the city\'s calendar at Motiv UG on December 19.</p> <div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">On the calendar</p><ul><li><span class="k">Oct 24</span><span class="v">Kampala Jazz & Soul Benefit — UMA Show Grounds</span></li><li><span class="k">Nov 8</span><span class="v">Levixone — Kampala Serena Hotel</span></li><li><span class="k">Nov 13</span><span class="v">Mesach Semakula: 50 Years of Greatness — Kingdom Kampala</span></li><li><span class="k">Nov 19-22</span><span class="v">Nyege Nyege Festival — Kalagala Falls, Jinja</span></li><li><span class="k">Dec 4</span><span class="v">Chameleone Unlimited — Cricket Oval, Lugogo</span></li><li><span class="k">Dec 11</span><span class="v">Nina Roz — Lugogo Hockey Grounds</span></li><li><span class="k">Dec 19</span><span class="v">Afro Netion: Wave Edition — Motiv UG</span></li></ul></div> <p>There is a second, less comfortable story inside this calendar. Promoter and TV host Douglas Lwanga said flatly this month that "Uganda\'s entertainment industry is no longer profitable"; dancehall artist Kapeke apologised after walking off the Roast & Rhyme stage citing sound problems; Tracy Melon cut a Mbarara set short after a fan\'s behaviour. More shows do not automatically mean better-paid artists — a tension the industry\'s own numbers back up.</p> <p class="note">Dates and venues are taken from organiser listings (evento.ug, UGEvent, AllEvents); always confirm with the promoter before travelling. <em>Credit: evento.ug; UGEvent; AllEvents; RioTimes; Sqoop; Patro Uganda.</em></p>' },
    { id: 30, slug: 'blankets-and-wine-2026-season', category: 'Events', title: 'Blankets & Wine at 17: what Nairobi\'s festival model still teaches East Africa', excerpt: 'Three editions a year, a campaign about taste, and 17 years of Sundays on the grass — Kenya\'s flagship picnic festival remains the benchmark the region measures its live scene against.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: 'images/articles/blankets-and-wine-2026.jpg', imgCaption: 'An afternoon on the lawn at Blankets & Wine (illustration: 7H Music Blog)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
      body: '<p class="lead">Seventeen years in, Blankets & Wine is still the benchmark East African promoters measure themselves against.</p> <p>Kenya\'s flagship picnic festival confirmed a three-edition 2026 season in Nairobi — <strong>June 7</strong>, <strong>September 6</strong> and <strong>December 20</strong>, all at Laureate Gardens inside the Moi International Sports Centre at Kasarani. The year is being delivered under a new campaign, "Your Taste Lives Here", launched on April 14 at Hit Labzz during the festival\'s first Storytellers Lab: taste as identity rather than luxury, expressed through music, food and creative enterprise.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>A line-up that reads like a regional map</h3> <p>The June edition put Kenyan Afro-fusion vocalist Labdi, genge king Mejja, Mordecai (Dexx), Serro, Mutoriah and Watendawili on the main stage alongside Nigerian singer <strong>Fave</strong>. The expanded Onja Onja Stage — the festival\'s electronic and alternative platform — added South Africa\'s Goldmax to a roster of Kenya-based acts (Hiribae, DJ IV, LA Dave, Suraj, Sir M) across Afro-house, amapiano and gqom. "Onja Onja has always been the home of the vibes at the festival," creative director <strong>Muthoni Ndonga</strong> said at the announcement. "This evolution is about going deeper, giving the stage its own identity and the freedom to push the sound forward."</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The lesson inside the June controversy</h3> <p>When organisers moved the June 7 edition indoors to the Kasarani Indoor Arena because of a rain forecast, Nairobi pushed back hard — because the festival\'s whole promise is a Sunday on grass with a blanket and a glass of wine, and the shift broke it. Seventeen years of consistent format has made the audience protective of the thing itself, which is a compliment as much as a complaint. December 2025\'s edition showed what the formula can carry: Scorpion Kings (Kabza De Small and DJ Maphorisa), Tanzania\'s Marioo and the UK\'s Donae\'o on stage, with the Onja Onja Market giving Kenyan fashion, food and craft brands a paying audience. The festival has even exported the format, debuting in Bradford, UK, under the British Council\'s UK/Kenya Season.</p> <p>Kampala\'s equivalent energy currently arrives in a different shape: sponsor-backed concert nights — Chameleone at Lugogo, Levixone at Serena, Mesach Semakula at Kingdom Kampala (see <a href="#article/kampala-live-season-chameleone-levixone-mesach">the season calendar</a>) — plus one four-day festival on the Nile. What Nairobi has that Kampala does not yet is a repeatable quarterly format: the same field, the same tone, a date people plan their year around. The December 20 edition is the next test of it.</p> <p class="note">Line-ups and tickets are published at blanketsandwine.com. <em>Credit: Music In Africa; Capital Lifestyle; KenyanVibe; KBC; Nairobi Events Guide.</em></p>' },
    { id: 31, slug: 'east-africa-streaming-2026-gen-z-amapiano', category: 'Industry Insights', title: 'East Africa\'s streaming wave: Gen Z, amapiano and the end of one national sound', excerpt: 'Spotify\'s Nairobi numbers put 18-24s behind 53.7% of streams while Bongo Flava, gengetone, gospel and amapiano all grow double digits — and the region\'s borders keep dissolving.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: 'images/articles/east-africa-streaming-wave.jpg', imgCaption: 'A DJ set in East Africa (illustration: 7H Music Blog)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
      body: '<p class="lead">The most interesting thing about East African listening in 2026 is that no single genre owns it any more.</p> <p>Spotify\'s internal data for Nairobi, published in June, put listeners aged 18-24 behind <strong>53.7%</strong> of all streams in the Kenyan capital — and then showed what those young listeners do with the app: Bongo Flava grew 75%, gengetone 48%, gospel 37%, amapiano 34%, R&B 28% and Afrobeats 25%. Not one national sound, but a shuffle between them.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Amapiano stopped being South African</h3> <p>The clearest regional story is what East Africa did with amapiano after importing it. Tanzanian producers slowed the log drums under Swahili hooks; Kenyan artists pushed Sheng punchlines into the pocket; Ugandan Afro-pop acts used the rhythm as a bridge back to big choruses. South Africa\'s Scorpion Kings headlined Blankets & Wine\'s December 2025 edition in Nairobi, Tanzanian star Marioo shared the same stage, and Ugandan promoters booked Boohle for a Kampala party — the traffic runs both ways. Producers are crossing borders too: Al Xapo\'s "SNOKONOKO VI" with <a href="#article/joshua-baraka-recess-ii-braka-land">Joshua Baraka</a> took a South African amapiano blueprint and gave it an East African vocal.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The gatekeepers changed</h3> <p>Radio still matters — the Atlas data puts radio listeners at more than double the live-event attendance of non-listeners (<a href="#article/uganda-music-economy-atlas-report-2026">the numbers are here</a>) — but the first filter for a new song is now a playlist, a TikTok clip or a DJ set. Spotify has leaned into that with Culture Club, its editor-led podcast on African music, recorded live at the company\'s Greasy Tunes sessions in Nairobi in July with Kenyan producer Eric Musyoka; its episodes cover Nigerian hip-hop, South Africa\'s Lekompo movement, East Africa\'s new wave and the role of women in amapiano.</p> <p>For artists the upside is reach without permission: a Kampala act can build a Nairobi audience without a Kenyan label or a radio plug. The catch is monetisation, and it is severe — Uganda\'s streaming penetration sits near 1.1% against a regional average of five to ten per cent, so the audiences arriving on the phone are still worth a fraction of what they are elsewhere.</p> <p class="note">Growth figures are Spotify\'s June 2026 Nairobi data as reported in August 2026. <em>Credit: Spotify (Culture Club / Greasy Tunes coverage); OkayAfrica; Vumbi Ventures; Music In Africa.</em></p>' },
    { id: 32, slug: 'uganda-music-economy-atlas-report-2026', category: 'Industry Insights', title: 'The numbers behind Uganda\'s music economy — and why independence is the default', excerpt: 'A national report puts 89.7% of Ugandan artists outside any label, 27% earning nothing, and streaming penetration at 1.1%. The picture is tough — and it explains almost everything about how the scene behaves.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: 'images/articles/uganda-music-economy.jpg', imgCaption: 'The business end of Ugandan music (illustration: 7H Music Blog)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
      body: '<p class="lead">A single report explains almost everything about how Uganda\'s music scene behaves.</p> <p>The Atlas report, published in January 2026, is best known for naming Joshua Baraka\'s "Wrong Places" the country\'s top song of 2025 — Fik Fameica and Liam Voice\'s "Biggwa" followed at 11, Vinka, Nwagi and Ava Peace\'s "Batuleke" at 12, Elijah Kitaka\'s "Ekyange" at 13, with Azawi, Bebe Cool, Rema Namakula and Winnie Nwagi all inside the top 30. But the numbers around the chart are the more important part.</p> <div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">The industry in numbers</p><ul><li><span class="k">89.7%</span><span class="v">of Ugandan artists work independently — no label, no capital, no infrastructure</span></li><li><span class="k">27%</span><span class="v">earn nothing at all from their music</span></li><li><span class="k">1.1%</span><span class="v">streaming penetration in Uganda, against a regional average of 5-10%</span></li><li><span class="k">2.1×</span><span class="v">more live-show opportunities for artists who promote on radio</span></li><li><span class="k">66% vs 31%</span><span class="v">regular radio listeners who attend six or more events a year, versus non-listeners</span></li><li><span class="k">3:1</span><span class="v">women outperform men on brand deals — the only income path where they do</span></li></ul></div> <p>Read together, those figures describe a fast-moving, highly entrepreneurial scene with almost no infrastructure under it. Nine in ten artists are their own label, so every release is self-funded; radio, not streaming, is still the switch that turns a song into paid live work; and brand partnerships — where women out-earn men three to one — are the most reliable non-show income in the business.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The reform argument</h3> <p>The report landed in a year of public argument about the industry\'s foundations. In April, Uganda Communications Commission bans on certain songs reignited a debate about how — and whether — music should be regulated, with NTV following the fallout. Musician Elijah Kitaka called the scene "1500 years behind" and asked for copyright training rather than sympathy; Karole Kasita argued Uganda has failed to develop its own identifiable sound; Sheebah Karungi\'s copyright case with her former Team No Sleep camp went to court; and the Uganda National Musicians Federation\'s funding arguments spilled into public. Meanwhile Spice Diana, David Lutalo and others have spoken openly about what the economics actually feel like.</p> <p>For independents the report is less a verdict than a map: keep release costs low, treat live and brand work as the revenue, and own everything you can. It is the version of the industry most Ugandan artists are already living in.</p> <p class="note">Figures as reported by Pulse Uganda in January 2026 and NTV in April 2026. <em>Credit: Atlas report via Pulse Uganda; NTV Uganda; UgandaOnline; Howwe.</em></p>' },
    { id: 33, slug: 'african-music-global-october-2026', category: 'News', title: 'From the Grammys to October: African music\'s year on the world stage', excerpt: 'Tyla made category history in February, Fela Kuti received a posthumous Lifetime Achievement Award, and this month the world\'s release calendar carries South African jazz and a Ugandan singer trading hooks with Lagos.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: 'images/articles/african-music-global-2026.jpg', imgCaption: 'Africa on the world stage (illustration: 7H Music Blog)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
      body: '<p class="lead">African music\'s year on the world stage started with a historic Grammy night and has not slowed down since.</p> <p>At the <strong>68th Grammy Awards</strong> on February 1, South Africa\'s Tyla won Best African Music Performance for "Push 2 Start" — her second win in the category after "Water" in 2024, making her the first artist to take it twice, and the third straight year a woman has won it, following Tems\' "Love Me JeJe" in 2025. She beat a shortlist that included Burna Boy\'s "Love", Davido and Omah Lay\'s "With You", Ayra Starr and Wizkid\'s "Gimme Dat" — and Uganda\'s Eddy Kenzo, nominated with Mehran Matin for "Hope & Love". The night before, Fela Kuti was honoured with a posthumous Lifetime Achievement Award; Trevor Noah hosted the ceremony for the sixth and final time.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>What October looks like</h3> <p>The current month carries the same signature in a quieter key. Blue Note released <em>L I N D A</em>, the second label album from Durban saxophonist <strong>Linda Sikhakhane</strong> and his fifth overall, produced by Nduduzo Makhathini — spiritual jazz with township bones, and a reminder that Africa\'s global exports are not only pop. Pop-wise, the traffic keeps moving between Lagos, Johannesburg and East Africa, with our own <a href="#article/joshua-baraka-tiwa-savage-commando">Uganda-Nigeria collaboration of the season</a> still ahead.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The other half of the story</h3> <p>International recognition and domestic economics are not the same story. The same season that produced a Grammy win came with Ugandan data showing 27% of local artists earning nothing from music and streaming penetration stuck at 1.1% (<a href="#article/uganda-music-economy-atlas-report-2026">the numbers are here</a>). A nomination travels further than a royalty cheque. Both facts are true in 2026 — which is precisely why the next five years of African music will be fought over infrastructure, not awards.</p> <p class="note">Grammy details as reported in February 2026; <em>L I N D A</em> release dates per Blue Note / Universal listings. <em>Credit: OkayAfrica; Music In Africa; Guardian Nigeria; AllMusic; Premium Times.</em></p>' },
    { id: 34, slug: 'joka-sky-blue-to-no-condition', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Joka, the Afro-pop shapeshifter behind "SU CASA"', excerpt: 'Six releases deep and still reworking his own sound, Joka has moved from the rap-adjacent records of 2022 to the Afro-pop of "SKY BLUE" and this year\'s "NO CONDITION" — always with his own name on the writing credit.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: 'images/articles/joka-kampala-afropop.jpg', imgCaption: 'Kampala at dusk (illustration: 7H Music Blog)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/joka/1620053754',
      body: '<p class="lead">Joka has never stayed in one lane long enough for a listener to catch up.</p> <p>The Kampala Afro-pop singer and songwriter — <strong>Ntale Joram Emmanuel</strong> in the credits, and his name is on every one of them — has spent five years releasing small, deliberate projects at roughly one a year, each one a little further from the last. The lane keeps moving; the handwriting stays his. On Apple Music he is filed under Afro-Pop, but the catalogue runs from soft rap over drum programming to full Afro-beat production, and his best records tend to be the ones that sit between the two.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The run</h3> <p>It starts with <em>Louder Actions</em>, a five-track EP in May 2022, and a dense first year of singles: "Beamer" and "Outlander" with the producer and label owner <strong>GEOXwill</strong>, "Monster", "Lovesick" with Mxlan A, "Stay", "FEELS LIKE SUMMER" with Drillz the Rapper and, that October, "Andele" with Heimech. The following year he stretched out: "S K I N D E E P" in February, "Cigar" with Ledra in April, "Bandit" with Thy\'Medi and Chicor in September, "Differently" with Soundlykbb in November and "December" with GEOX the Producer and ItzDanny at the end of the year — all of it orbiting the ten-track <em>O U T S I D E</em> album he released in August 2023.</p> <p>Then he sharpened. <em>TO LOVE IS TO HATE</em> landed in March 2024 — seven tracks, with "NIGHTS IN RIYADH", "FADE" and "RUSH" among them — followed by <em>New Wave</em> in November and the six-track <em>SKY BLUE</em> at the end of that December, which gave him his three most-streamed songs: <strong>"GUZINAZONTO"</strong>, <strong>"MASAALI"</strong> and <strong>"TELL EM NO"</strong>. "I Miss You", his duet with <a href="#article/amanda-moon-afro-pop-singles">Amanda Moon</a>, came from the same sessions.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>This year: "NO CONDITION"</h3> <p>His newest release is a change of gear. <strong>"NO CONDITION"</strong>, out on April 24, 2026 through PALAGROOVE and RAIN.LABS, sounds like a reset: the drums sit further back, the voice is doing more of the work, and there is nothing on the record that a producer could not have built in a bedroom. It has been holding steady in the top five of his Apple Music listings since. Along the way he has also become a dependable guest — "SU CASA" with Ogezi and GEOXwill, "Deliver" with X-tmore te and Jam Vybz, "RED PEPPER" with DNK, and features on <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a>\'s and GEOXwill\'s own projects.</p> <div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">Joka, on record</p><ul><li><span class="k">Name</span><span class="v">Ntale Joram Emmanuel — Kampala, Uganda</span></li><li><span class="k">Projects</span><span class="v"><em>Louder Actions</em> 2022 · <em>O U T S I D E</em> 2023 · <em>TO LOVE IS TO HATE</em> · <em>New Wave</em> · <em>SKY BLUE</em> 2024</span></li><li><span class="k">Latest</span><span class="v">"NO CONDITION" — April 24, 2026 (PALAGROOVE / RAIN.LABS)</span></li><li><span class="k">Start with</span><span class="v">"GUZINAZONTO", "MASAALI", "TELL EM NO", "SU CASA"</span></li><li><span class="k">Frequent collaborators</span><span class="v">GEOXwill / GEOX the Producer, Ogezi, Thy\'Medi, DNK, Soundlykbb, Amanda Moon</span></li></ul></div> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The "Munakampala" line</h3> <p>The one piece of self-description he has given a platform is a sentence in his Apple Music biography about "Munakampala", the song he calls the record he always wanted to write — "from a place of confusion, anxiety and of course love." That is a fair reading of everything else too: songs about the city, written by somebody who is honest about the fact that the city is complicated.</p> <p>He has no album to his name yet, no press cycle, and — as far as we can verify — no Spotify profile on which to hear him, which is why this page links to Apple Music instead. What he has is a catalogue that has quietly grown to more than thirty songs, all written by him, released on independent labels, and still moving. If you know the correct Spotify page for the Ugandan Joka — the Ugandan one, not the Polish act that shares his name — send it to us and we will wire it up.</p> <p class="note">Fresh Find: he is on Apple Music as Joka, and his newest single is in the Releases grid above. <em>Credit: Apple Music; Deezer; MusicBrainz; Boomplay; Audiomack.</em></p>' },
    { id: 23, slug: 'nana-nyadia-afro-fusion-spotlight', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Nana Nyadia, Afro-fusion from Kampala to Johannesburg', excerpt: 'A Nyege Nyege debut, a home at Party Wright Records, a first cohort nod at the AUMEX accelerator — and a catalogue that began with a 2022 heartbreak. Meet the Afro-fusion and soul voice turning her story into songs.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: PHOTO('ab7a444fa9b2900f9c54fea6'), imgPos: 'center 25%', imgCaption: 'Nana Nyadia (photo: Spotify artist profile)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '2SzY2nJnBs8mzh2jgqOmdL', title: 'Nana Nyadia — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/nana-nyadia/1766006884',
      body: '<p class="lead">Nana Nyadia writes about the space between two homes — and sings it like she is still standing in the middle of it.</p> <p>Born in Uganda with South Sudanese roots and raised between Kenya and Uganda, the singer and songwriter now based in Kampala works in Afro-fusion and soul: Afrobeats rhythm underneath a voice that carries more than the beat lets on. Her own description is the plainest one going — "Afro-Fusion &amp; Soul artist ... crafting culture-bridging stories" — and it is the most accurate summary anyone has written for her.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>It started with "Sad Song"</h3> <p>She has traced her way into music to a song called "Sad Song", written out of a 2022 heartbreak and the mental-health struggles that came with it. A 2024 Daily Monitor profile followed that thread: an artist who found the studio through the hardest year she had had, then a 23-year-old student of Graphics and Design at Isbat University. Her writing name is Nyadia Fiona, and the credits carry it — her own name on every release, alongside producer TK DICOCO on "Habibi".</p> <p>The records keep the mood. "STAND FOR" and its acoustic version arrived in September and October 2024, followed by "Habibi" that December. Then came "Mpenzi" in August 2025 and "Feeling" that November — both built with producer Melanin Boy — and "Not Promised" in May 2026 with Single Dee, a duet whose Shazam credits pair Nyadia Fiona with Deng James Aleu, and which South Sudanese "best of the year" playlists picked up within weeks.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Nyege Nyege, and a label family</h3> <p>She made her Nyege Nyege debut in 2024 — the festival that has become the scene\'s annual proving ground — and in December 2025 she was the guest on The Ugandan Boy Talk Show, where the conversation ran from her Ugandan, South Sudanese and Kenyan background to the debut EP she has been building, <em>Sour Valentine</em>. Nothing has been announced since, which leaves it filed firmly under "one to watch".</p> <p>Her records land through Party Wright Records, the Kampala label whose circle also includes <a href="#article/ayma-gone-ass-titude">AYMA</a> and reaches South Sudan through Himaya Music.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Johannesburg, June 2026</h3> <p>In June, New Vision reported that Nana Nyadia was part of the first AUMEX Artist Accelerator cohort at the Africa Rising Music Conference in Johannesburg, alongside Mbeewo, Vini Mercy, <a href="#article/amanya-mosquitoes-alte-rnb">Amanya</a> and Matt Travers — a continental nod for a catalogue that is still only a handful of singles deep.</p> <p>That is the story so far: a writer who kept choosing the softer, harder take, and a run of songs that sound like travel. Start with "Not Promised", then let "Feeling" run. <em>Credit: Daily Monitor; New Vision; The Ugandan Boy Talk Show; Audiomack; Shazam; Spotify; Apple Music.</em></p>' },
    { id: 24, slug: 'tai-dai-whet-new-wave-spotlight', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Tai Dai and the new-wave rap of "WHET"', excerpt: 'Rapper, singer, songwriter — Aseru Thandiwe Dralega has been on everyone else\'s record for three years. The five-track "WHET" EP is the moment the catalogue turned and pointed at her.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: PHOTO('933f78e29342953dc434151a'), imgPos: 'center 25%', imgCaption: 'Tai Dai (photo: Spotify artist profile)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '77G1P2NHQOnx0leyvpN3ym', title: 'Tai Dai — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/tai-dai/1426402603',
      body: '<p class="lead">Uganda\'s new wave has a lot of names. Tai Dai has spent the last few years making sure hers is one you learn properly.</p> <p>Born Aseru Thandiwe Dralega, she works as a rapper, singer and songwriter — her given names turn up in the writing credits — and describes her sound as the place where hip-hop, alté, Afrobeats and UK garage meet. That is a broad claim until you hear the catalogue, which moves from Kampala rap to something closer to pop melody without ever changing voice.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The long apprenticeship</h3> <p>Her earliest credit is a guest spot on Lagum the Rapper\'s "UNA" (2020). "D.O.n (Pull up)" with Prnz and Ni\'dario followed in 2022, and then the collaborations stacked up: "Thursday" with Kaboo and <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a> in January 2024 on Home Crowd Recordings, "Boy Scout" with mwami and Lagum the Rapper that June, and the five-track "EA OPEN" EP with Agent Mgumbe and mwami in September 2024. Along the way she has appeared on Axon and Soundlykbb\'s "Ni**as In Fashion" from <em>CARTEL BIZNESS</em>, Maya Amolo\'s "Last Time", Kavali King\'s "Pristine", Ninja C\'s "On The Road" and, in July 2026, Mxestro\'s "Okugwa mu Deep End".</p> <p>Then came "Outside Shoes" in May 2025 — the record she unpacked at length on The Ugandan Boy Talk Show\'s Boxing Day episode, in a conversation that ran from that song to Nyege Nyege and to the women holding down Ugandan hip-hop.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>WHET</h3> <p><em>WHET</em>, her five-track EP, arrived on July 23, 2026 under her own Tai Dai Official imprint, and it is the clearest statement yet: "GRANTED" opens it, "Uh Huh" — already out as a single in April — supplies the hook, "CHEST (Interlude)" breaks it in half, and "Where U Go" and "UNFORGETTABLE" close it out. Short records, sharp edges, no filler.</p> <p>What it adds up to is an artist the scene already trusts — the one who is on everybody\'s song before she is on her own cover. <em>WHET</em> is where that finally flips. <em>Credit: Apple Music; Spotify; Deezer; Genius; The Ugandan Boy Talk Show.</em></p>' },
    { id: 25, slug: 'ayma-gone-ass-titude', category: 'News', title: 'AYMA links with Soundlykbb on "GONE" — then drops "ASS TITUDE"', excerpt: 'The Party Wright Records singer has had a full 2026: "GONE" with Soundlykbb, a run of features across Kampala and Juba, and a brand-new solo single out today.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '4 min read', img: PHOTO('c979068e88b7ac2e998501a7'), imgPos: 'center 25%', imgCaption: 'AYMA (photo: Spotify artist profile)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '45p8xCFhR0gkXgzyWW0Wos', title: 'AYMA — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/ayma/1818880233',
      body: '<p class="lead">AYMA keeps good company — and this year the company has kept getting bigger.</p> <p>In September 2026 the Kampala Afro-beat singer joined <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a> on "GONE", a short, buoyant record that pairs his Entebbe-rooted hook-making with her lighter, higher register. It has since settled at the top of her Spotify top-tracks list.</p> <p>Today she adds to it. "ASS TITUDE" is a solo single released through Party Wright Records under exclusive license to Himaya Music — the same Kampala-to-Juba axis that carries <a href="#article/nana-nyadia-afro-fusion-spotlight">Nana Nyadia</a> — and it follows June\'s "MAPENZI" in a run that has quietly become one of the steadier ones in that label family.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The run behind it</h3> <p>AYMA arrived in 2025 with "FOUND", an R&amp;B-leaning debut on her own AYMA MUSIC imprint, then "Go Down" that August and "Only" that October. The features widened the frame: Tojo\'s "Fall In Love" and Party Wright\'s "Intentional Love" in 2026, plus VTM Records\' "Over" and "MELODIES" and E-FROST\'s "Nyege Nyege" with ZEUS BNM in October.</p> <p>She is still early — a few thousand monthly listeners, no album, no press run — which is exactly the point of a Fresh Find: get in before the roll-out. <em>Credit: Spotify; Apple Music; Party Wright Records; Himaya Music.</em></p>' },
    { id: 26, slug: 'amanda-moon-afro-pop-singles', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Amanda Moon, the Afro-pop name letting the singles speak', excerpt: '"Mandem", "Ready for You", "I Miss You" with Joka, "No More Tears" with Ric Keys — a small, self-released Afro-pop catalogue with no press kit and hooks that keep travelling.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '4 min read', img: PHOTO('82dfa55279af253d03303c21'), imgPos: 'center 25%', imgCaption: 'Amanda Moon (photo: Spotify artist profile)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '5lqRfQ6FpBufPWHpXy8HeO', title: 'Amanda Moon — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/amanda-moon/1746973428',
      body: '<p class="lead">Some artists announce themselves. Amanda Moon just keeps turning up in the playlist.</p> <p>Her catalogue is short and entirely self-released — every one of her own singles carries her name in the phonographic copyright line — and it has grown slowly since "Mandem" in May 2024, an Afro-pop record with a hook that refuses to sit still. "I Miss You" followed that June as a duet with Joka, then "Ready for You" in December, and "No More Tears" with Ric Keys at the end of 2025.</p> <p>There is no press kit to quote from and no interview to lean on. What there is, instead, is a consistent sound: Afrobeats drums under a soft, slightly wistful vocal, and songs built to get to the chorus. Filed under Afro-Pop and Afrobeats wherever her records are stocked, she has also worked as a guest — most notably on mista dayvis\'s "Bestie" in April 2025, and on Joka\'s "I Miss You" from the year before.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Where to start</h3> <p>Take "Mandem" first, then "I Miss You" — the duet is the one that shows how well her voice sits against somebody else\'s. Then "No More Tears", the most recent thing under her own name. Four singles in two years is not a roll-out. It is a slow, deliberate build, and it is working. <em>Credit: Spotify; Apple Music.</em></p>' },
    { id: 22, slug: 'remz-down-new-wave-spotlight', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Remz, Uganda\'s newest Afro-pop voice', excerpt: 'A 2026 run of features with Kyle Simbwa and Mayanja, and a small, self-released catalogue topped by a brand-new single called "Down" — meet one of the freshest names in Ugandan music.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: PHOTO('10ecdc4f3b8d78baa50b80e1'), imgPos: 'center 25%', imgCaption: 'Remz (photo: Spotify artist profile)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '2UJWgPQKyu8nm5eHE2WAL8', title: 'Remz — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/remz/1873055478',
      body: '<p class="lead">Every music scene has a name you hear first in somebody else\'s song. In Uganda right now, one of those names is Remz.</p> <p>Remz is a new-wave Ugandan artist singing in the space between Afro-pop and R&amp;B. Her voice does the work quietly — light where the beat is heavy, unhurried where everything else rushes. Hers is a catalogue you arrive at sideways: a hook on another artist\'s record, a two-minute song that ends before you are ready, and then you go looking for more.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Start with "Down"</h3> <p>Her newest single, <em>Down</em>, arrived on September 25, 2026. Two minutes and ten seconds, no guest, no showing off — just a low, steady vocal over a soft Afro-pop bed. It is the clearest introduction to what she does, and it currently sits at the top of her Spotify artist page.</p> <p>What is striking is how much of her story runs through features. Remz keeps turning up in other people\'s songs, and more often than not she is the part you remember.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The features</h3> <p>In January she released "Do You Wrong" with <a href="#article/kyle-simbwa-apple-music-up-next">Kyle Simbwa</a>, the Apple Music Up Next East Africa artist who spent this year building toward <em>East African Summer</em>. The pairing works because neither of them pushes: Simbwa\'s Afropop glide and Remz\'s R&amp;B restraint meet in the middle, and the whole thing is over in two minutes and nineteen seconds. A smart first move for an artist with no big machine behind her.</p> <p>Then there is the live thread running through the rest. "SLOWLY" comes from <em>STAY</em>, the six-track June EP by Mayanja — a two-and-a-half-minute Afro-beat duet that sounds best after dark. And "Jade", with EDavid Magori, is the slow, warm one you end a night on.</p> <ul class="winners"><li><span class="k">Down</span><span class="v">Solo single · Sep 25, 2026 · 2:10</span></li><li><span class="k">Do You Wrong</span><span class="v">with Kyle Simbwa · Jan 28, 2026 · 2:19</span></li><li><span class="k">SLOWLY</span><span class="v">from Mayanja\'s <em>STAY</em> EP · Jun 19, 2026 · 2:38</span></li><li><span class="k">Jade</span><span class="v">with EDavid Magori · 2:48</span></li></ul> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Why she is on this page</h3> <p>The new wave of Ugandan music does not wait for a label to hand it a push. Remz is releasing under her own name, singing on other people\'s records and building a catalogue one short song at a time, with the writing credited to her and her co-writers. Nothing about it feels staged, and that is exactly the point.</p> <p class="note">Fresh Find: her whole catalogue is four songs, all under three minutes. The ▶ button on this page plays her top tracks on Spotify — start with "Down", then "Do You Wrong".</p> <p>Remz is on Spotify and Apple Music. <em>Credit: Spotify; Apple Music; Shazam; Deezer.</em></p>' },
    { id: 21, slug: 'joshua-baraka-recess-ii-braka-land', category: 'News', title: 'Joshua Baraka: "Recess II" lands November 12, then B\'RAKA LAND comes home', excerpt: 'Spotify\'s countdown is live for the seven-track EP, with "YoYo", "what do i know" and "Commando", a collaboration with Tiwa Savage. Sixteen days later comes his homecoming show at Lugogo Hockey Grounds.', date: 'Oct 15, 2026', readTime: '8 min read', img: 'images/articles/joshua-baraka-brakaland.jpg', imgPos: 'center 30%', imgCaption: 'Joshua Baraka on the Brakaland Tour in Mbarara (photo: Sqoop)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '50zFt2TgGXsfDnf6W0gYys', title: 'YoYo — Joshua Baraka (from Recess II)' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/yoyo-single/6797879229',
      body: '<p class="lead">It\'s official: Joshua Baraka\'s new EP, <em>Recess II</em>, arrives on <strong>November 12, 2026</strong>. Spotify has opened pre-saves with a live countdown. Sixteen days later, the Kawempe-born star plays the biggest homecoming show of his career.</p> <p>Spotify\'s tracklist preview shows seven songs on the sequel to 2024\'s <em>Recess</em>. Both of his 2026 solo singles are there, with "YoYo" at track 3 and "what do i know" at track 5. The other titles are still hidden, but Baraka has revealed the biggest one himself. On October 11 he teased "Commando", a collaboration with Nigerian Afrobeats star <strong>Tiwa Savage</strong>. Pulse Uganda and Sqoop report it as the EP\'s second track. There is no standalone release date for the song yet.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The road to Recess II</h3> <p>"what do i know", his first solo single of 2026, landed on July 1. "YoYo" followed on August 26, a song about two people stuck between friendship and something more. He sings it in English, Luganda, French and Lingala. Baraka wrote and produced it himself, with extra production from Hendrix Sam, and it has already passed 1.1 million Spotify plays. Both singles are released under his own name, under exclusive licence to Moves Recordings.</p> <p>Before that came a run of collaborations. In February he released a "Morocco" remix with Jamaican dancehall star Shenseea and Axon. In April came "AYAYAAH" with Rwanda\'s Element EleéeH and Kenya\'s Bien, and in June "YOU" with Khid Ceejay. His back catalogue keeps growing too. The JAE5-produced "Wrong Places" has passed 29.5 million plays on Spotify, and "No Bad Vibes", his collaboration with Jazzy, Gyakie and Kilimanjaro, has passed 235 million. He now draws about 1.6 million monthly listeners.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>"It\'s East Africa"</h3> <p>Baraka has always thought regionally. "From the start, I didn\'t want to just stay in Uganda," he told Joey Akan on OkayAfrica\'s <em>Afrobeats Intelligence</em> in May. He remembered taking buses from Kampala to Kenya and Rwanda to push his music. "When I talk of East Africa, I talk about it in a block, because to me, it\'s not just one place. It\'s not just Uganda… it\'s East Africa." He was blunt about the comfort trap at home, too: "If you blow up in Uganda, they don\'t really try to go out there and get together with other artists or take their music out there. You\'ll still make money, you still have deals, you\'ll still be good."</p> <p>That approach has taken him to the Afro Nation stage and sold-out shows in Europe and Australia. It also earned him a nomination for Best African Music Act at the 2026 MOBO Awards.</p> <figure><img src="images/articles/joshua-baraka-mbale.jpg" alt="Joshua Baraka performing at Bandali Lounge in Mbale" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>On the Brakaland EA Tour: Baraka at Bandali Lounge, Mbale. Photo: Nile Post.</figcaption></figure> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Brakaland on the road</h3> <p>Before the EP, he took the show on the road. The Brakaland EA Tour is his first nationwide roadshow. It is backed by Johnnie Walker, which recently named him its "Icon Walker". The tour visited Gulu, Arua, Mbale and Mbarara. At Bandali Lounge in Mbale he opened with "What Do I Know" and closed with "YoYo". "I think if they are looking for his number one fan across the world, I would come in first," one reveller told New Vision. At Nkore Place in Mbarara he came on at about 1am and played "Nana", "YoYo", "Dive In", "This Time", "Wrong Places", "Dreams", "Morocco" and "Dalilah". "There is no song of his that I don\'t know or stream," one fan told Sqoop.</p> <p>"Thank you, Mbarara, for showing up and showing me so much love," Baraka said that night. "We\'ve taken Brakaland across Uganda, and now it\'s time to bring it home. Kampala, we\'re coming for you. I\'ll see you at the Hockey Grounds on November 28 for the grand finale."</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The finale: Lugogo, November 28</h3> <div class="factbox"><p class="factbox-title">B\'RAKA LAND East Africa</p><ul><li><span class="k">When</span><span class="v">Saturday, November 28, 2026. Gates open at 12pm</span></li><li><span class="k">Where</span><span class="v">Lugogo Hockey Grounds, Kampala</span></li><li><span class="k">Tickets</span><span class="v">UGX 75,000 general admission · UGX 120,000 VIP · UGX 2,000,000 per table, at <a href="https://brakaland.ug/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">brakaland.ug</a></span></li></ul></div> <p>The official site promises "one full day in the heart of Kampala". DJs, food and friends start at noon, and the main show begins at sundown. It\'s a big step up from a year ago. On November 29, 2025, Baraka played his first-ever concert, <em>Into The Braka Land</em>, at the Ndere Cultural Centre, presented by Talent Africa Group. This year the name returns on a festival-sized ground, after a full tour and with a new EP to sing.</p> <blockquote>"This year has tested me in more ways than one. There have been a lot of ups and downs, but I\'m grateful for it all. You have showed me more love than I deserve through everything, and all I can do is give you music and shows. I love you all so much. Uganda, get ready. B\'raka is coming home."<cite>Joshua Baraka</cite></blockquote> <p>Pre-save <em>Recess II</em> on <a href="https://open.spotify.com/prerelease/1LxUYn2jTWDyBlDmyKPcvs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spotify</a>, and learn the words before the 28th. <em>Credit: Spotify; brakaland.ug; Pulse Uganda; Sqoop; New Vision; Nile Post; PML Daily; MBU; OkayAfrica (Afrobeats Intelligence); Album Talks; Talent Africa Group; Apple Music.</em></p>' },
    { id: 20, slug: 'ug-hip-hop-awards-2025-winners-2026', category: 'News', title: 'UG Hip Hop Awards: every 2025 winner, and how to enter #UGHIPHOPAWARDS26', excerpt: 'Pryce Teeba and Recho Rey took the top rapper prizes, the Soundlykbb-featuring "Ni**as in Fashion" won Song of the Year and The Mith won Album of the Year. Here are all the winners, the stories behind them, and how to enter the next edition.', date: 'Oct 14, 2026', readTime: '7 min read', img: 'images/articles/ughha-2025-launch.jpg', imgCaption: 'the UG Hip Hop Awards \'25 press launch (photo: Nile Post)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
      body: '<p class="lead">Ugandan rap has its own night of the year. The UG Hip Hop Awards (UGHHA) set out to give "a fair platform for all Ugandan Hip-hop acts", celebrating "the past, present and the future of the genre in Uganda." The official site has already switched its banner to <strong>#UGHIPHOPAWARDS26</strong>.</p> <p>The last ceremony was held on Friday, December 5, 2025, at the International University of East Africa (IUEA) auditorium in Kampala. Old-school pioneers and new-wave rappers filled the room. Bond 7 whisky was lead sponsor, with Pepsi and NRG Radio as partners, and every adult with an ordinary ticket got a free 200ml bottle at the door. "The Uganda Hip Hop Awards speaks directly to the nation\'s creative energy," said Hillary Baguma, UBL\'s brand manager for mainstream spirits. Hip-hop veterans Babaluku, Navio and Lyrical G attended the launch, where project manager Nathan Mubembe said the awards keep the genre\'s "continued flow into the mainstream."</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Every 2025 winner</h3> <p class="note">The official site lists these as the 9th-edition winners. Some press coverage of the night called it the 8th.</p> <ul class="winners"><li><span class="k">Male Rapper of the Year</span><span class="v"><a href="#article/pryce-teeba-hip-hop-afrobeats">Pryce Teeba</a></span></li><li><span class="k">Female Rapper of the Year</span><span class="v">Recho Rey</span></li><li><span class="k">Song of the Year</span><span class="v">Axon &amp; Denim Cartel, "Ni**as in Fashion" ft. <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a> &amp; Tai Dai</span></li><li><span class="k">Album of the Year</span><span class="v">The Mith, <em>The Ugandan 2</em></span></li><li><span class="k">Mixtape of the Year</span><span class="v">Timcence, <em>No Skips Allowed</em></span></li><li><span class="k">Rap Fusion of the Year</span><span class="v">Timcence, "Can Ayeba"</span></li><li><span class="k">Collaboration of the Year</span><span class="v">Eno Hip Hop Cypher (Eno Hip Hop / Konkrete Music, ft. Ruyonga, Navio, Keko, Kvan, Lagum, J-Wats, Da Agent, Vkaycee, Afraw, The Homie &amp; AJ Stylz)</span></li><li><span class="k">Sweet 16 (Verse of the Year)</span><span class="v">Navio, <em>Eno Hip Hop Cypher</em></span></li><li><span class="k">Lyricist of the Year</span><span class="v">Tucker HD, "Just Left Ntinda"</span></li><li><span class="k">Producer of the Year</span><span class="v">Rukidi Beatz, "Power"</span></li><li><span class="k">Rookie of the Year</span><span class="v">Son Of Aboda, "Ayee"</span></li><li><span class="k">Video of the Year</span><span class="v">Kavali ft. UNITY, "YA SALAWO"</span></li><li><span class="k">Inspirational Song of the Year</span><span class="v">Barna ft. Karungi, "Victory"</span></li><li><span class="k">Central Rapper of the Year</span><span class="v">Vudu Child, "Wanjaulo" ft. Drop Pin, Lany X &amp; OG Stash</span></li><li><span class="k">Eastern Rapper of the Year</span><span class="v">TIZ MC OMUBEDE, "Fallen Souls Mu Silimu Freestyle"</span></li><li><span class="k">Northern Rapper of the Year</span><span class="v">Stayput, "Jo Kare Ni" ft. Toobi Smolz</span></li><li><span class="k">Western Rapper of the Year</span><span class="v">Truth256, "Party"</span></li><li><span class="k">Diaspora Act of the Year</span><span class="v">J Baller UG, "Don Corleone (Remix)"</span></li><li><span class="k">Deejay of the Year</span><span class="v">DJ Ata</span></li><li><span class="k">Media Personality of the Year</span><span class="v">NUVEYLIVE</span></li><li><span class="k">Lifetime Achievement Award</span><span class="v">Jeff Ekongot</span></li></ul> <h3 ' + H3 + '>The storylines</h3> <p><strong>Pryce Teeba\'s year.</strong> The <a href="#article/pryce-teeba-hip-hop-afrobeats">Pyong Recordz founder</a> won Male Rapper of the Year, the title Navio held in 2024. His 14-track album <em>Marinate</em> followed the same month. <strong>Recho Rey, full circle.</strong> Rachael Mirembe first broke through at the 2017 UG Hip Hop Awards as the only woman in the MTN cypher competition. She went on to win Female Rapper and Rookie of the Year in the MTN era, and in 2025 she was named Female Rapper of the Year again.</p> <p><strong>Doubles.</strong> Timcence took both Mixtape and Rap Fusion of the Year. Konkrete Music\'s Eno Hip Hop Cypher won Collaboration of the Year, and Navio\'s verse on it won the Sweet 16. <strong>The Mith keeps winning.</strong> After Lyricist and Mixtape of the Year in 2024, he took Album of the Year for <em>The Ugandan 2</em>. NUVEYLIVE kept Media Personality of the Year. The night\'s emotional high point was a standing ovation for Jeff (Geoffrey) Ekongot as he received the Lifetime Achievement Award.</p> <p><strong>The 7H connection.</strong> Song of the Year went to "Ni**as in Fashion", Axon and Denim Cartel\'s record featuring Fresh Find <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a> and Tai Dai. Tai Dai also guests on "Temptation" by <a href="#article/sente-afro-fusion-storyteller">Senté</a>, whose debut single "DND" earned him two UGHHA nominations, for Rap Fusion and Rookie of the Year.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Rewind: 2024</h3> <p>The previous edition named Navio Male Rapper and Ninja C Female Rapper of the Year. Ruyonga\'s "Parte Yani" won both Song and Rap Fusion of the Year, and Rickman Manrick\'s <em>NdiMuto</em> took Album of the Year. Trappex Booming was Producer of the Year, Nsokwa was Rookie of the Year, and MC Yallah received the Lifetime Achievement Award.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>How to enter in 2026</h3> <p>No date or nominee list for the 2026 edition (the 10th, by the official site\'s count) has been published yet. The process is public, though. The organisers announce the nomination window on their social media, and it stays open for one month. Artists, labels and managers submit through <a href="https://www.ughha.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ughha.com</a> or a shared link, and the work must have been released within the eligibility period they publish. Nominees are announced afterwards, and the ceremony is held in Kampala and broadcast live on social media and TV.</p> <p>Follow <a href="https://www.instagram.com/officialughha/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@officialughha</a> so you don\'t miss the call for entries. Questions go to <a href="mailto:officialughha@gmail.com">officialughha@gmail.com</a>. <em>Credit: ughha.com; Pulse Uganda; The Independent; SoftPower News; allAfrica; Nile Post; MBU; HipHopAfrican; MTN Uganda.</em></p>' },
    { id: 19, slug: 'echoled-kush-after-hour', category: 'Interview', title: 'Echoled on "Kush After Hour": a late-night EP that shows his range', excerpt: 'The singer behind "Mangadalena" wrote all six songs on his second EP, moving between dancehall, Afro-R&B and Afro-pop. He has also been named a festival headliner and added Joshua Baraka\'s former manager to his team.', date: 'Oct 14, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: 'images/articles/echoled-kush-after-hour.jpg', imgCaption: 'Echoled (photo: Nile Post)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '4Mg8jCywOiQ4qDE7Xz419U', title: 'Kush After Hour — Echoled' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/kush-after-hour-ep/6800323697',
      body: '<p class="lead">In just over a month, Echoled released a new EP, was named a festival headliner and got a management upgrade. Ibunbaazi Ssentongo has made himself one of the busiest names in Ugandan music.</p> <p>His music draws on Afrobeat, dancehall, R&amp;B and Afro drill. Nile Post describes the blend as Afro-R&amp;B and dancehall with a touch of urban <em>kadongo kamu</em>, the storytelling tradition of Ugandan guitar music. The audience is growing fast. He now has more than 40,000 monthly listeners on Spotify, where "Mangadalena" leads his catalogue with over 150,000 plays, ahead of "New Love", "Embilizi" and "Home Alone". "Mangadalena", "Broken Hearts" (released on Valentine\'s Day 2026) and "Embilizi" are all getting heavy airplay.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Inside Kush After Hour</h3> <p>Released on August 28, <em>Kush After Hour</em> follows his 2025 EP <em>It Speaks</em>. Echoled told Nile Post that the idea came straight from conversations with his listeners. He noticed that many fans connected most with his music during their "Kush moments", so he set out to make a body of work for that time. He describes the result as sensational, soothing and energetic, and a move beyond conventional dancehall.</p> <p>He wrote all six songs himself. Aiz Fix My Mix and Jacob Beats produced the EP, building the beats from scratch. The variety is deliberate. The title track blends R&amp;B and dancehall, "Sitimila" goes Afro-R&amp;B and "Embilizi" leans Afro-pop, with "Dirty Veronica", "Kinte" and "Angelica" completing the six. People have tended to tie him to the Afro-pop feel of "Mangadalena", he said, and this project is his chance to show other sides of his artistry while staying an East African artist at heart.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Headliner, with a new team</h3> <p>The Afro Migration Festival picked him to headline its 2026 edition, three days of African music, art and culture at Nile Nest Resort in Jinja over the Independence weekend (October 9–11). "Echoled represents the new wave of Ugandan music — authentic, energetic and deeply connected to the culture," the festival team said when they announced him.</p> <p>Behind the scenes, Lobstar has joined his management team. He is the former manager of <a href="#article/joshua-baraka-recess-ii-braka-land">Joshua Baraka</a> and widely credited with a hand in the "YoYo" singer\'s success. Lobstar will work alongside Echoled\'s parent management company, MVP Management. Echoled released a steady stream of singles before the EP: "One Night Stand" (December 2025), then "Broken Hearts", "Kintu" and "Home Alone" in 2026. The momentum is real.</p> <p>Start with "Mangadalena", then let <em>Kush After Hour</em> play through, preferably late. <em>Credit: Nile Post; allAfrica; MBU; Spotify; Apple Music.</em></p>' },
    { id: 18, slug: 'sente-afro-fusion-storyteller', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Senté, Kampala\'s Afro-fusion storyteller', excerpt: 'A Spotify Fresh Finds Africa pick, a two-time UG Hip Hop Awards nominee and, in Deeds Magazine\'s words, "at the fore" of East Africa\'s alt-R&B rise. Meet the voice behind "Let It Play" and "Teasin\'".', date: 'Oct 13, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: PHOTO('dd7fc0b970e14650b8db45f4'), imgPos: 'center 30%', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '28f5KCRxUeYsOEegevfKrj', title: 'Teasin\' — Senté' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/teasin-ep/1823792749',
      body: '<p class="lead">"With Alt R&amp;B fashioned as the \'it\' genre in East Africa currently, Senté is at the fore of its ascent." Deeds Magazine wrote that when it named the Kampala artist one of ten East African creative innovators to know.</p> <p>Senté calls himself an Afro-fusion artist. His own bio describes music that blends "hip-hop lyricism, RnB-inspired melodies and afro-centric rhythms". Deeds praises his "velvety melodies" and "cinematic narrative-writing" at the meeting point of R&amp;B, hip-hop and Afro-pop. Spotify files him under Alté and Afro-soul, and his own playlists sign off as "Man Like Senté".</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>A debut that got noticed</h3> <p>His first single, "DND" with Denesi and Chxf Barry (December 2023), earned two UG Hip Hop Awards nominations, for Rap Fusion and Rookie of the Year. Two months later came "Let It Play", a cross-border duet with Kenya\'s Maya Amolo produced by Yannique and Shress. With more than 135,000 Spotify plays, it is still his most-played song. "Kampala Woman" and his debut EP <em>You Wasted Kampala Love</em> followed in March and April 2024. In May that year, Spotify selected him for Fresh Finds Africa. BBC 1Xtra, Deeds Magazine, Tangaza Magazine and COLORSxSTUDIOS have all featured his music.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Teasin\' and the 2025 run</h3> <p>He built toward his second EP with a single every couple of months: "Twista" in March 2025, "Temptation" with Tai Dai in April and "Typo" with Azooora in June. <em>Teasin\'</em>, a five-track EP released with Platoon, followed on July 23, 2025.</p> <p>He\'s also one of the new wave\'s most dependable guests. You\'ll find him on <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a>\'s "CASINO", Irxnzi\'s "Sun Don\'t Shine" and Geinixx\'s "COCO". He also appears on "DINNER" with La Soülchyld, kalibwani and Joka, and on "Somebody" from <a href="#article/mike-mungu-new-project">Mike Mungu</a>\'s <em>LOVE ISNT ENOUGH</em>. Spotify\'s "fans also like" list puts him next to Chxf Barry, Baru and <a href="#article/tungi-bebe-to-life-of-sound">Tungi</a>, the heart of Kampala\'s new wave.</p> <p>Start with "Let It Play", then go straight into <em>Teasin\'</em>. He\'s on Instagram at <a href="https://www.instagram.com/sente4ever/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@sente4ever</a>. <em>Credit: Spotify; Deeds Magazine; Apple Music; Shazam.</em></p>' },
    { id: 17, slug: 'amanya-mosquitoes-alte-rnb', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Amanya, the Kampala bedroom producer behind "MOSQUITOES"', excerpt: 'Her voice moves through Alté, R&B, pop and Afro-house. With the "Buri Izooba" EP alongside Mozaka and DJ Spinny, plus "MOSQUITOES" and its new Tipcey remix, Amanya is one of Kampala\'s quiet new finds.', date: 'Oct 13, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: PHOTO('d16877d11a3a292e6f40a7cd'), imgPos: 'center 30%', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '7CudWV8gnBMZx9v3wfDjnS', title: 'MOSQUITOES — Amanya' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/mosquitoes-single/1885660665',
      body: '<p class="lead">Amanya calls herself an "African musical alchemist" from metropolitan Kampala. In her own words, her voice "threads through Alté, R&amp;B, Pop and Afro-House, exploring the tension between love, rejection, and a quiet desire to belong."</p> <p>She\'s a true do-it-yourself artist: she produces in her own room. Her bio lists collaborators including Mozaka, DJ Spinny, RiC.KEYS, Ogeezy, Villagio Media and PALAGROOVE, a sign of how far her bedroom sessions already travel.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>From the dancefloor…</h3> <p>Amanya started 2026 on the club side. <em>Buri Izooba</em>, credited to Mozaka, Amanya &amp; DJ Spinny, came out on March 24 on Dbakka Studios under exclusive licence to WeTalkSound. The four-track EP stretches the song out with a six-minute original, a radio edit with Café Riddim, an instrumental and a stripped-back "Vocals" version. Deezer\'s credits list Amanya as the sole writer of the vocal version. That week, WeTalkSound included the release in its WeTalkFridays new-music roundup.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>…to the slow burn</h3> <p>Then came "MOSQUITOES" on April 9, released through PALAGROOVE / RAIN. LABS. Her bio describes it as an Alté R&amp;B and soul fusion, and Apple Music files it under Alté. With more than 18,000 plays, it is her most-played song on Spotify. Her "Discovered on" list there includes the editorial playlists New Music Friday Kenya, R&amp;Beats and afro &amp; soul. On September 25 she gave the song a second life with "MOSQUITOES (Remix)" featuring Tipcey. She also released the single "Neiwe" this year.</p> <p>With fewer than 3,000 monthly listeners, Amanya is still an early find, and that\'s exactly why she\'s on this page. Start with "MOSQUITOES", then turn it up for <em>Buri Izooba</em>. <em>Credit: Spotify; Apple Music; Deezer; WeTalkSound.</em></p>' },
    { id: 1, slug: 'smokie-cee-joins-7h-island-kiss', category: 'Press Release', title: 'Smokie Cee joins the 7H roster with "Island Kiss"', excerpt: '7H welcomes Afrobeat and Afro-pop artist Smokie Cee to the family. His single "Island Kiss", featuring Baranga, is out now everywhere.', date: 'Oct 12, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: BASE + 'cover-island-kiss.jpg', author: { name: '7H Music Group', role: 'Press Office', avatar: BASE + 'artist-smokie-cee.jpg' }, listen: { type: 'track', id: '5d6GZiK7C86C4V2H6qKZcC', title: 'Island Kiss — Smokie Cee ft. Baranga' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/album/island-kiss-feat-baranga-single/6804911850',
      body: '<p class="lead">7H Music Group is proud to welcome Afrobeat and Afro-pop artist Smokie Cee to the family.</p><p>His single <em>Island Kiss</em>, featuring Baranga, is out now on all platforms — following the pair\'s 2025 link-up on <em>Confirm</em>. The track blends sun-soaked Afrobeat grooves with a melodic hook built for the dancefloor, and marks Smokie Cee as the fourth signed artist on the 7H roster, joining Baranga, Mr Fallback and Mike 1ne.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>A New Voice in the House</h3><p>"He\'s got that rare thing — a voice you recognise after one bar," says the label. The single is available now on Spotify and Apple Music, with the official audio and artwork circulating across 7H\'s social channels (Instagram @7hmusicgroup, X @7hmusicgroup).</p><p>Stream <em>Island Kiss</em> now and follow Smokie Cee\'s journey as the newest member of the house. <em>Credit: 7H Music Group press office.</em></p>' },
    { id: 2, slug: 'baranga-debut-album-chapters-i-never-wrote', category: 'Press Release', title: 'Baranga releases debut album "Chapters I Never Wrote"', excerpt: 'The full-length project is out now on all platforms, with Mr Fallback among the features — the most complete statement yet from the 7H camp.', date: 'Oct 8, 2026', readTime: '4 min read', img: BASE + 'cover-chapters.jpg', author: { name: '7H Music Group', role: 'Press Office', avatar: BASE + 'artist-baranga.jpg' }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '1A3yUmi9B5GRwY0PjeQTMp', title: 'Chapters I Never Wrote — Baranga' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/album/chapters-i-never-wrote/6768973076',
      body: '<p class="lead">Baranga\'s debut album <em>Chapters I Never Wrote</em> is out now on all platforms.</p><p>The full-length project features Mr Fallback among others and represents the most complete statement yet from the 7H camp — blending Afro-pop, rap and street storytelling across a cohesive body of work. It follows the breakout success of <em>Badman</em>, which passed 290,000 streams and landed Baranga on Uganda\'s biggest Spotify playlists and the Ugandan Top 100.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>From Breakout to Album</h3><p>Where <em>Badman</em> (feat. Stando & Andrinah) introduced Baranga to a wider audience, <em>Chapters I Never Wrote</em> widens the frame — pairing anthemic singles like <em>Party Riddim</em> with deeper cuts that show his range as a writer. Production is handled in-house by Mike 1ne, who mixed and mastered the project.</p><p>Listen now on Spotify and Apple Music. <em>Credit: 7H Music Group press office.</em></p>' },
    { id: 3, slug: 'mr-fallback-kiwatto', category: 'Press Release', title: 'Mr Fallback returns with new single "Kiwatto"', excerpt: 'Fresh off "Ndi Solidi Mu" and "Abeyoo", Mr Fallback keeps the run going with another infectious Afro-pop cut made for the dancefloor.', date: 'Oct 3, 2026', readTime: '4 min read', img: BASE + 'cover-kiwatto.jpg', author: { name: '7H Music Group', role: 'Press Office', avatar: BASE + 'artist-mr-fallback.jpg' }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '4ynVnzXIhbYeGQc8Z6cMt7', title: 'Kiwatto — Mr Fallback' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/album/kiwatto-single/1896243349',
      body: '<p class="lead">Mr Fallback keeps his hot streak alive with new single <em>Kiwatto</em>.</p><p>Fresh off <em>Ndi Solidi Mu</em> and <em>Abeyoo</em>, the Afro-pop rising star delivers another infectious, dance-ready cut built for East Africa\'s new wave. Produced in-house by Mike 1ne, <em>Kiwatto</em> is out now on Spotify and Apple Music, joining a run of singles that has made Mr Fallback one of the most consistent voices on the 7H roster.</p><p>His melodic, dance-ready records — from <em>Ndi Solidi Mu</em> to <em>Party Riddim</em>-adjacent collaborations — capture the spirit of East Africa\'s new wave, and <em>Kiwatto</em> continues that momentum. <em>Credit: 7H Music Group press office.</em></p>' },
    { id: 4, slug: 'badman-290k-streams', category: 'Press Release', title: '"Badman" passes 290K streams as Baranga hits the Ugandan Top 100', excerpt: 'The breakout single keeps climbing, landing Baranga on Uganda\'s biggest Spotify playlists and marking him as one to watch.', date: 'Sep 25, 2026', readTime: '3 min read', img: BASE + 'cover-badman.jpg', author: { name: '7H Music Group', role: 'Press Office', avatar: BASE + 'artist-baranga.jpg' }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '1naYEYjsM85p1bKdygXurU', title: 'Badman — Baranga ft. Stando & Andrinah' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/album/badman-feat-stando-andrinah-single/1812327603',
      body: '<p class="lead">Baranga\'s breakout single <em>Badman</em> has passed 290,000 streams.</p><p>The track continues to climb Uganda\'s Spotify charts, landing Baranga on the country\'s biggest playlists and the Ugandan Top 100 — marking him as one of the most exciting new voices in East African music. The single, featuring Stando and Andrinah, is available now on Spotify and Apple Music.</p><p>Stream <em>Badman</em> now and dive into the rest of <em>Chapters I Never Wrote</em>. <em>Credit: 7H Music Group press office.</em></p>' },
    { id: 5, slug: 'meet-mike-1ne', category: 'Interview', title: 'Meet Mike 1ne — the sound behind 7H', excerpt: 'The label\'s in-house producer and sound engineer steps up with his own music: the EP "Love Look What You Made Me Do" and single "Dance Di Night Away" are out now.', date: 'Sep 18, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: BASE + 'artist-mike-1ne.jpg', imgPos: 'center 25%', author: { name: '7H Music Group', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BASE + 'artist-mike-1ne.jpg' }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '4DA5LBW4NWzVLcVSOrBLsI', title: 'Mike 1ne — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/artist/mike-1ne/1840503931',
      body: '<p class="lead">"If it comes out of 7H sounding like that, Mike 1ne is the reason."</p><p>Mike 1ne is the label\'s in-house music producer and sound engineer, and an admin of the house. He shapes the 7H sound from the studio — mixing and mastering the releases, running sessions for the roster, and helping steer the business day to day. He is credited on Baranga\'s <em>Chapters I Never Wrote</em>, Mr Fallback\'s <em>Kiwatto</em>, and the Tungi collaboration <em>Siteesa</em>, among others.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>Stepping Out Front</h3><p>Now he\'s stepping up with his own music: the EP <em>Love Look What You Made Me Do</em> and the single <em>Dance Di Night Away</em> are out now on Spotify and Apple Music. Studio sessions and production bookings are open — reach him at <a href="mailto:mikeonerecords@gmail.com">mikeonerecords@gmail.com</a>. <em>Credit: 7H Music Group.</em></p>' },
    { id: 6, slug: 'review-baranga-chapters-i-never-wrote', category: 'Album Review', title: 'Review: Baranga — "Chapters I Never Wrote"', excerpt: 'A confident, cohesive debut that cements Baranga as one of Uganda\'s most compelling new voices.', date: 'Oct 10, 2026', readTime: '7 min read', img: BASE + 'cover-chapters.jpg', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '1A3yUmi9B5GRwY0PjeQTMp', title: 'Chapters I Never Wrote — Baranga' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/album/chapters-i-never-wrote/6768973076',
      body: '<p class="lead">Baranga\'s debut album <em>Chapters I Never Wrote</em> is the most complete statement yet from the 7H camp.</p><p>Across the project, the Afro-pop/rap standout blends street storytelling with melodic hooks, with Mr Fallback among the features. The production — handled in-house by Mike 1ne — is warm, punchy and radio-ready without losing its edge. Standout cuts include the breakout <em>Badman</em> and the title track, which ties the album\'s themes together.</p><p>It\'s a debut that sounds like an artist who already knows who he is — and a defining Kampala record of 2026. <strong>Rating:</strong> 8.5 / 10. <em>Credit: 7H Music Blog editorial.</em></p>' },
    { id: 7, slug: 'rise-of-independent-music-east-africa', category: 'Industry Insights', title: 'The Rise of Independent Music in East Africa', excerpt: 'How Kampala, Nairobi, and Dar es Salaam are building a new model for artist ownership and global reach.', date: 'Sep 28, 2026', readTime: '8 min read', img: 'images/articles/independent-collage.jpg', imgCaption: 'Baranga, Mr Fallback, Mike 1ne and Soundlykbb', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
      body: '<p class="lead">A decade ago, breaking out of East Africa meant leaving it. Today, the region\'s independent artists are building global audiences from home.</p><p>Streaming platforms, social media, and a new generation of local labels — 7H Music Group among them — have reshaped the economics of music in the region. Artists retain ownership, control their masters, and reach listeners in Lagos, London, and Los Angeles without a major-label middleman. Rolling Stone Africa has highlighted this momentum, noting Ugandan acts like Soundlykbb, Elijah Kitaka, Azawi, CXNRVD and Kohen Jaycee alongside the wider scene.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>The 7H Model</h3><p>At 7H, the philosophy is simple: artist-first deals across three divisions — 7H Records, 7H Management, and 7H Publishing — with transparent royalties and long-term publishing partnerships. "We\'re not here to own artists," says the label. "We\'re here to amplify them."</p><p>The challenge now is infrastructure — better live venues, stronger collection societies, and fairer streaming payouts. But the momentum is unstoppable. <em>Credit: 7H Music Blog editorial; Rolling Stone Africa.</em></p>' },
    { id: 8, slug: 'beyond-7h-ugandas-new-wave', category: 'Interview', title: 'Beyond 7H: Uganda\'s new wave of independent voices', excerpt: 'A round-up of the artists, producers and songwriters shaping Kampala\'s sound outside the major-label system.', date: 'Sep 14, 2026', readTime: '7 min read', img: 'images/articles/new-wave-collage.jpg', imgCaption: 'Pryce Teeba, Kyle Simbwa, Mike Mungu and Soundlykbb', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
      body: '<p class="lead">Kampala\'s music scene has never been louder — or more independent.</p><p>From Afro-pop and hip-hop to R&B and amapiano, a new generation of artists is building audiences on their own terms, often with nothing more than a home studio and a SoundCloud link. This blog exists to cover all of them — 7H artists and the wider scene alike.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>Fresh Finds</h3><p>Alongside 7H\'s Baranga, Mr Fallback, Mike 1ne and Smokie Cee, keep an eye on Fresh Finds like <a href="#article/sente-afro-fusion-storyteller">Senté</a>, <a href="#article/amanya-mosquitoes-alte-rnb">Amanya</a>, <a href="#article/echoled-kush-after-hour">Echoled</a>, <a href="#article/pryce-teeba-hip-hop-afrobeats">Pryce Teeba</a>, <a href="#article/kyle-simbwa-apple-music-up-next">Kyle Simbwa</a>, <a href="#article/mike-mungu-new-project">Mike Mungu</a>, <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a>, <a href="#article/ali-boomaye-new-single">Ali Bomaye</a> and <a href="#article/remz-down-new-wave-spotlight">Remz</a> — the emerging voices rising out of Ntinda, Entebbe and beyond. Got a story or a release we should cover? Reach out at <a href="mailto:7hmusicgroupug@gmail.com">7hmusicgroupug@gmail.com</a>. <em>Credit: 7H Music Blog editorial.</em></p>' },
    { id: 9, slug: 'kyle-simbwa-apple-music-up-next', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Kyle Simbwa — Apple Music\'s Up Next artist for East Africa', excerpt: 'The Ugandan vocalist, producer and multi-instrumentalist is celebrated for blending 90s R&B, 80s synth-pop and Afrobeats into a distinctive Alté sound.', date: 'Oct 11, 2026', readTime: '7 min read', img: 'images/articles/kyle-simbwa-up-next.jpg', imgPos: 'center 30%', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '5YA97S9AzbC0pbTaeX3X8W', title: 'East African Summer — Kyle Simbwa' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/east-african-summer/6797513622',
      body: '<p class="lead">Kyle Simbwa has been named Apple Music\'s Up Next artist for East Africa — a major milestone for Ugandan and East African music on the global stage.</p><p>The 27-year-old from Kasangati, a town near Kampala, is celebrated for blending \'90s R&B, \'80s synth-pop, A.O.R. and Disney TV influences with Afrobeats and Hip-Hop, creating a distinctive Afrobeat/Alté sound. "Being selected for Up Next East Africa by Apple Music means a lot because I\'m part of a generation of African artists building something uniquely ours," Simbwa said. "I want people to hear my music, feel where I\'m from, and see how far it can travel."</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>Hands-On Craft</h3><p>Deeply involved in every stage of his music, Simbwa works across songwriting, production, recording, mixing and engineering, giving him the freedom to experiment. His growing live presence includes headline performances at Xpressions and appearances at Afro Urban alongside Mike Kayihura and Kohen Jaycee. His 2025 debut project <em>Person</em> and singles like "Mwana Gwe" and "East African Girl" established him as a rising force, and his seven-track EP <em>East African Summer</em> (2026) explores love, nightlife and youth culture. In January he lent his voice to <a href="#article/remz-down-new-wave-spotlight">Remz</a> on "Do You Wrong" — one of the year\'s quietest and best link-ups.</p><p>"2026 is about taking everything I\'ve been building and pushing it further," he said. <em>Credit: Nyakundi Report; KBC; Global Publishers; Shazam. Apple Music Up Next East Africa.</em></p>' },
    { id: 10, slug: 'tungi-bebe-to-life-of-sound', category: 'Interview', title: 'Tungi: from "Bebe" to "Life of Sound" — a Kampala Afrobeat story', excerpt: 'Mutungi Keith, the Ugandan singer-songwriter blending Afrobeat, hip-hop and R&B, and his journey from a 2020 debut single to a growing catalogue.', date: 'Oct 6, 2026', readTime: '7 min read', img: PHOTO('a2aa524a55ea76758747e1c0'), imgPos: 'center 30%', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '4ppzhpslWW0PcByjJBLaKt', title: 'Life of Sound — Tungi' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/life-of-sound/1778800460',
      body: '<p class="lead">Tungi — real name Mutungi Keith — is a Ugandan singer and songwriter born and raised in Uganda, emerging as a prominent figure in the Afrobeat, hip-hop and R&B scenes since the early 2020s.</p><p>His entry into music was shaped by childhood in Uganda, where music played a central role; early memories of singing for peers ignited his passion. His debut single "Bebe" (August 28, 2020) marked his first foray into Afrobeat and urban sounds, followed by the <em>Pearl of Africa</em> EP series (2022–2023) and <em>Life of Sound</em> (2024). Influenced by Wizkid, J Hus and others, Tungi blends Afrobeat rhythms with hip-hop and R&B, exploring love, self-discovery and Ugandan urban life.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>Collaborations & Reach</h3><p>He has collaborated with Joshua Karuhanga on "Bandale" and with Mike 1ne on <a href="#article/tungi-mike-1ne-siteesa">"Siteesa"</a> (2026), and appears on Kyle Simbwa\'s <em>East African Summer</em> ("Sugar Honey"). His catalogue spans EPs like <em>Rush</em>, <em>Free Time</em> and singles such as "Wonder", "G Energy" and "Mademoiselle". "My dream is for my music to travel worldwide," he says. <em>Credit: The Culturing; Grokipedia; Apple Music; Deezer.</em></p>' },
    { id: 11, slug: 'tungi-mike-1ne-siteesa', category: 'Press Release', title: 'Tungi & Mike 1ne link up on "Siteesa"', excerpt: 'The new collaboration blends Afro-pop bounce with Mike 1ne\'s in-house production touch.', date: 'Oct 6, 2026', readTime: '3 min read', img: BASE + 'cover-siteesa.jpg', author: { name: '7H Music Group', role: 'Press Office', avatar: BASE + 'artist-mike-1ne.jpg' }, listen: { type: 'track', id: '4GDB9tMylfRQavVoI6UOkq', title: 'Siteesa — Tungi & Mike 1ne' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/us/album/siteesa/1888289462?i=1888289464',
      body: '<p class="lead">Tungi teams up with 7H\'s in-house producer Mike 1ne on new single "Siteesa".</p><p>The collaboration pairs Tungi\'s melodic Afro-pop delivery with Mike 1ne\'s production, mixing and mastering — a cross-scene link-up that shows how Kampala\'s artists and producers are increasingly working together across label lines. "Siteesa" is out now on Spotify and Apple Music. <em>Credit: 7H Music Group press office.</em></p>' },
    { id: 12, slug: 'soundlykbb-spotlight', category: 'Interview', title: 'Spotlight: Soundlykbb — Entebbe\'s Afro-pop, hip-hop & R&B voice', excerpt: 'From viral freestyles to a UG Hip Hop Awards Song of the Year — the Entebbe artist blending Afro-pop hooks, hip-hop bars and R&B melodies.', date: 'Oct 2, 2026', readTime: '6 min read', img: 'images/articles/soundlykbb.jpg', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '2izHnGuv9KzbPbfX6KsoTG', title: 'Soundlykbb — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/soundlykbb/1690023792',
      body: '<p class="lead">Soundlykbb makes music that refuses to stay in one lane — Afro-pop hooks, hip-hop bars and R&B melodies, often in the same song.</p><p>Born and raised in Entebbe, he first caught attention online with his freestyles, and the 2020 lockdown turned SoundCloud uploads into a real following. His debut EP <em>MADE IN ENTEBBE</em> (2023), made with long-time friend and producer Khxli, was followed the same year by <em>ZAABU</em> — Luganda for "gold" — and in 2024 by <em>SOUND, Vol. 1</em> and <em>Proof of Life</em> with Baru.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>Afro-Pop, Hip-Hop & R&B</h3><p>That range runs through his biggest records: "Thursday" with Kaboo and Tai Dai, "It All Depends" with I-Blade, "CASINO" and "Softer Landing" with Tungi and Kasvin. His collaboration with producer Axon and Denim Cartel, "Ni**as In Fashion", won Song of the Year at the 2025 UG Hip Hop Awards, and Rolling Stone Africa has named him among Uganda\'s standout artists. His latest release, "GONE" with AYMA, arrived in September 2026.</p><p>Soundlykbb\'s music carries his Entebbe roots into a sound built for listeners everywhere — and he\'s only getting started. <em>Credit: Spotify; Apple Music; RecentMusic; Rolling Stone Africa; UG Hip Hop Awards.</em></p>' },
    { id: 13, slug: 'pryce-teeba-hip-hop-afrobeats', category: 'Interview', title: 'Pryce Teeba: hip-hop, Afrobeats and the records keeping him independent', excerpt: 'The Kampala heavyweight — UG Hip Hop Awards Artist of the Year in 2017 and Male Rapper of the Year in 2025 — on live energy, Pyong Recordz, the album "Marinate", and a 2026 run that has already delivered "Nze Muno", "Winner" and "Route 66".', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '9 min read', img: PHOTO('654ac7ec9a32b4cb36e98746'), imgPos: 'center 25%', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Contributor', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '7LZeZZQTTiqOuxCN30dlGX', title: 'Pryce Teeba — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/artist/pryce-teeba/1129257218',
      body: '<p class="lead">"When the crowd moves, that\'s the whole point," says Pryce Teeba.</p><p>Pryce Teeba (aka BabyJajja), born November 3, 1987, is a rapper and songwriter from Kampala whose records sit where hip-hop meets Afrobeats — hard-hitting Luganda bars over grooves made to move a room. His career spans from 2012: his first mixtape <em>Kiwedde</em> (July 2014) introduced him to the Ugandan hip-hop scene, and his lead single "Side Zeno" won Song of the Year at the inaugural UG Hip-Hop Awards. He followed with <em>Kambajulire</em>, collaborations with Patrobas, TuckerHD, Byg Ben, Ruyonga and BigTril, and Artist of the Year at the 2017 UG Hip-Hop Awards. Later releases on Green Hit Records included "Mutima Gwo", "Lighter", "Obangayina" and "Plan B to A".</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>Pyong Recordz, "Ebivuga" and "Marinate"</h3><p>After a career shaped at Dustville Records, Pryce founded his own independent label, Pyong Recordz, rooted in Ntinda. Its most productive stretch came in 2025: the 12-track <em>Njaga la Sente</em> with Blinded Beats in May, the seven-track <em>Ebivuga</em> ("instruments") in July — where <a href="#article/ali-boomaye-new-single">Ali Bomaye</a> guests on "Nyege" — and then, in December, the 14-track album <em>Marinate</em>, which distils two decades of Ugandan hip-hop into one body of work. Its guest list reads like a scene roll-call: Lyrical G, The Mith, Navio, Babaluku, Spyda MC, Jenesis Kimera, Play01, Tucker HD and more. "For the Night", "Hiphop Mukyala", "All Day" and "Boyz from a Hood Near You" are the ones that stuck.</p><p>That December he was named Male Rapper of the Year at the <a href="#article/ug-hip-hop-awards-2025-winners-2026">UG Hip Hop Awards</a>, the title Navio had held in 2024.</p><h3 ' + H3 + '>The 2026 run</h3><p>He has not slowed down since. "Nze Muno", with Vibes Liper and Prod Bozo, arrived on March 20 through MADLUV Entertainment; "Winner" followed on May 1 with Roffe Wander and Blinded Beats. The features have kept coming too — Pryce appears on Troy Mc\'s <em>Cenozoic</em> EP (June 14) on "Route 66", on Vudu Child\'s "WIN WIN WIN" (January 30), on Quency\'s "Jus Sayin\'" freestyle in May, and on <em>THE NAVIO ORCHESTRA: LIVE IN KAMPALA</em> with "Abaana beeka" alongside Flex D\'Paper and Babaluku. On October 16 he surfaced again on Pascal Kalyabuli\'s "2026 (WORLDWIDE)".</p><blockquote>"There\'s so much talent here. The world just needs to listen properly."<cite>Pryce Teeba</cite></blockquote><p>Known for his live energy, Pryce remains a crowd favourite on Kampala stages — and he keeps the next generation in the room, pairing every Pyong Recordz release with a younger name. <em>Credit: Red Pepper (2020); Pyong Recordz; Apple Music; Spotify; UGHHA; The Ugandan Boy Talk Show.</em></p>' },
    { id: 14, slug: 'ali-boomaye-new-single', category: 'News', title: 'Ali Bomaye releases "Ali Bomaye the Ep"', excerpt: 'Six tracks, three languages and two features — "Piki Ponki" with Son Of Aboda and "Moko" with Chris King7 — as the Kampala Afro-beat artist steps out of the feature credits and onto his own cover.', date: 'Oct 16, 2026', readTime: '5 min read', img: 'images/articles/ali-bomaye-card.jpg', imgCaption: 'Ali Bomaye (artwork: Pyong Records · Soaw Media)', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'artist', id: '4H285cJrK0S0I7vtraU1Z3', title: 'Ali Bomaye — top tracks' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/ali-bomaye-the-ep-ep/6780491138',
      body: '<p class="lead">Ali Bomaye has spent this year letting the music do the talking. In July, that run became a project of his own.</p> <p><em>Ali Bomaye the Ep</em> is six tracks — "Fou De Toi", "Moko", "Juste Lá", "Piki Ponki", "Naleli" and "Zonga (Come Back)" — released through Pyong Records, Soaw Media and Ria Entertainment, the independent circle that also carries <a href="#article/pryce-teeba-hip-hop-afrobeats">Pryce Teeba</a>\'s Pyong Recordz. He writes his own material: all six songs carry his writing credit. And the language moves as fast as the mood does — English, Luganda and French, often inside the same verse.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Two features, two worlds</h3> <p>The guestlist shows the company he keeps. "Piki Ponki" brings in <strong>Son Of Aboda</strong>, the UG Drill breakout named Rookie of the Year at the <a href="#article/ug-hip-hop-awards-2025-winners-2026">2025 UG Hip Hop Awards</a>, who is now moving into Afro-rap ahead of his own November EP. "Moko" pairs Ali with Chris King7. Everything else is him alone — rare on a six-track debut.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>Where he fits</h3> <p>Before the EP there was "U Let Me Down" (November 2025), a Sirdepp single with Ali Bomaye and Omunubi, and "Nyege" on <em>Ebivuga</em>, where he trades lines with Pryce Teeba. Agaba Banjo\'s "Tega" followed in September. That thread — Afro-beat hooks, Ugandan rhythm, no rush — is what the EP formalises.</p> <h3 ' + H3 + '>What\'s next</h3> <p>Nothing has been announced, and that feels deliberate. Six tracks, three languages, one voice: a quietly confident introduction from an artist who has been in the room long enough to know exactly how he wants it to sound. Start with "Piki Ponki", then let "Naleli" run.</p> <p class="note">His artist profile is live on Spotify now — <a href="https://open.spotify.com/artist/4H285cJrK0S0I7vtraU1Z3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">follow Ali Bomaye there</a>, where the EP tracks lead his top list. Apple Music and the other major stores still carry everything.</p> <p>He publishes as Prïncë Dïämõnd on Instagram, <a href="https://www.instagram.com/ali.boomaye/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@ali.boomaye</a>. <em>Credit: Apple Music; Amazon Music; Deezer; UGHHA; Pulse Uganda.</em></p>' },
    { id: 15, slug: 'mike-mungu-new-project', category: 'Press Release', title: 'Mike Mungu announces new project', excerpt: 'The Kampala singer-songwriter teases an upcoming release and a run of live shows — and the album, "LOVE ISNT ENOUGH", is now out.', date: 'Sep 8, 2026', readTime: '4 min read', img: 'images/articles/mike-mungu.jpg', imgPos: 'center 30%', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR }, listen: { type: 'album', id: '4twrawLG16nxdplLiE6cBf', title: 'LOVE ISNT ENOUGH — Mike Mungu' }, apple: 'https://music.apple.com/ug/album/love-isnt-enough/6814441235',
      body: '<p class="lead">Mike Mungu is gearing up for his next move.</p><p>Born Munguriek Micheal (February 19, 2001) in Kampala, Uganda, Mike Mungu is a singer-songwriter blending pop, R&B, hip-hop and Afrobeats into what he calls "afro vocaloid" music. Inspired by Justin Bieber, Buju, Joshua Baraka, Ayra Starr and Mike Kayihura, he debuted with the single "MINE" and the EP <em>Finally</em> (2023), followed by "Maisha", "Kagina", "YOUR BODY", "Fallin" (2025) and "Treasure" with Ghana\'s Darkua. He has collaborated with Kirikou, Andyah and others.</p><p><strong>Update — Oct 2, 2026:</strong> the project is here. <em>LOVE ISNT ENOUGH</em>, a seven-track album, is out now on Spotify and Apple Music. <em>Credit: Apple Music; Spotify; Boomplay; Audiomack.</em></p>' },
    { id: 16, slug: 'east-african-music-goes-global', category: 'Industry Insights', title: 'East African music goes global: the new wave', excerpt: 'From Joshua Baraka to Soundlykbb, how Ugandan artists are reaching international audiences without leaving home.', date: 'Sep 1, 2026', readTime: '7 min read', img: 'images/articles/global-collage.jpg', imgCaption: 'Kyle Simbwa and Soundlykbb', author: { name: '7H Music Blog', role: 'Editorial', avatar: BLOG_AVATAR },
      body: '<p class="lead">East African music is having a moment on the global stage.</p><p><a href="#article/joshua-baraka-recess-ii-braka-land">Joshua Baraka</a>\'s viral hit "Nana" and follow-ups like the JAE5-produced "Wrong Places" (now past 29 million Spotify plays) have made him one of Uganda\'s biggest musical exports. Rolling Stone Africa has spotlighted a new generation — including <a href="#article/soundlykbb-spotlight">Soundlykbb</a>, Elijah Kitaka, Azawi, CXNRVD and Kohen Jaycee — while Apple Music\'s Up Next East Africa programme has elevated <a href="#article/kyle-simbwa-apple-music-up-next">Kyle Simbwa</a>. These artists blend local traditions with global genres, proving that Kampala can be both home base and launchpad.</p><p>As 7H Music Group and independent voices alike push forward, the question is no longer whether Ugandan music can travel — it\'s how far. <em>Credit: Rolling Stone Africa; SICKA VISION; Apple Music; Spotify.</em></p>' },
  ];

